er-field/id #uuid "0383e74a-ba27-5bf2-b541-84feb1f5b21c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "690a42b8-dcb3-555a-9d9d-b596c97be14f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332796" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)+3{t}^{2}+1} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)-2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}+3L{{t}^{2}}+L{1} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{{x}_{2}(t)}-2L{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,L{{t}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\n$$\n\n we can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)-\\frac{2}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point